{#
    The mark in the line that sends a reader to the foot of the page.

    The core prints `node.value`, which is what the author typed between the
    brackets — for `[#note]_` that is `#note`, and the block at the foot of the
    page calls the same footnote `[1]`. A reader looking for the note they just
    saw has nothing to match.

    The number is the target's and not the node's. A footnote written `[1]_`
    carries its own; one written `[#name]_` or `[#]_` is numbered while the
    document is compiled, which is the whole reason to write it that way, and
    until then the node says nought. The target is what the compiler hands back
    once it has counted — so it is asked first, and the node's own number is
    the fallback for the form that stated one.

    No class on the mark. It is a `<sup>` with a link in it, and the document
    layer already says what both of those are; a name here would be a hook in
    the markup with nothing behind it, which is the one thing this theme is not
    allowed to write.
#}
{%- if node.internalTarget -%}
    <sup>[<a href="{{- renderTarget(node.internalTarget) -}}">{{- node.internalTarget.number|default(node.number) -}}</a>]</sup>
{%- else -%}
    <sup>[{{- node.number -}}]</sup>
{%- endif -%}
